<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niels Bohr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niels Bohr was a Danish physicist who proposed the Bohr model of the atom in 1913, applying quantum ideas to the structure of electrons. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics in 1922. His institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, founded in 1921, became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum mechanics</a>. He escaped occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1943, advised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a> and later promoted the peaceful use of atomic energy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niels-bohr.jpg" alt="Niels Bohr" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The American Institute of Physics credits the photo [1] to AB Lagrelius &amp; Westphal, which is the Swedish company ... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niels_Bohr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Niels Henrik David Bohr was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> on 7 October 1885, the second of three children of Christian Bohr, Professor of Physiology at the University of Copenhagen, and Ellen Adler, daughter of the Danish Jewish banker David Baruch Adler. His younger brother Harald became a mathematician and played football for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> at the 1908 Summer Olympics. Niels enrolled at the University of Copenhagen in 1903 and studied physics under Christian Christiansen. In September 1911, with a Carlsberg Foundation fellowship, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, where he failed to impress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-j-thomson">J. J. Thomson</a>, and then to Manchester at the invitation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-rutherford">Ernest Rutherford</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Bohr proposed that the energy levels of electrons are discrete and that electrons revolve in stable orbits around the nucleus but can jump from one level to another. Later models have replaced it, though its underlying principles remain valid. He also conceived the principle of complementarity, that items can be analysed in terms of contradictory properties, such as behaving as a wave or as a stream of particles. This notion dominated his thinking in both science and philosophy.</p><p>In April 1917 he began a campaign for an Institute of Theoretical Physics, backed by the Danish government, the Carlsberg Foundation, industry and private donors. Legislation establishing it passed in November 1918, and he became its director. Now the Niels Bohr Institute, it was a focal point for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum mechanics</a> in the 1920s and 1930s, when most of the best-known theoretical physicists spent time there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1933 Bohr offered refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> temporary posts and financial support, and helped them find places, among them James Franck, Lise Meitner and Edward Teller. In September 1943 word reached him that the Nazis regarded his family as Jewish and that he was in danger of arrest. The Danish resistance helped him reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> by sea, and he persuaded King Gustaf V to make public Sweden's willingness to give asylum to Jewish refugees. Historians disagree on how far this led to the rescue of over 7,000 Danish Jews. He joined the British Tube Alloys project and was part of the British mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a>.</p><p>After the war Bohr called for international cooperation on nuclear energy and helped to establish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cern">CERN</a>. He died on 18 November 1962, and the element bohrium is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niels Bohr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Danish figures of wide influence include the philosopher Søren Kierkegaard, the writer Hans Christian Andersen and the physicist <b>Niels Bohr</b>, whose Copenhagen institute became a centre of quantum physics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Niels Bohr</b> is credited with finding inspiration in yin and yang symbolism, while Joseph Needham argued that the book had impeded scientific development.</blockquote><a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.68,12.57&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Copenhagen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4824 265 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Niels Bohr"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5274 535h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5274 535h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.68,12.57&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
